<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hoshang Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1435">1435</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Alp Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hoshang Shah was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, reigning from 1406 to 1435, son of the dynasty's founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilawar-khan-ghuri">Dilawar Khan Ghuri</a>. He moved the capital from Dhar to the hill fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>, which he fortified, and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states. His marble tomb at Mandu is considered the earliest marble building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoshang-shah.jpg" alt="Hoshang Shah" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drnsreedhar1959 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:001hoshang-1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hoshang Shah was born Alp Khan around 1375 and was also known as Arslan Khan before he took the title Hoshang Shah. His father, Dilawar Khan Ghori, had belonged to the court of Firuz Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, probably by Firuz. In 1401 Dilawar Khan made himself independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> for all practical purposes. When he died in 1406, Alp Khan succeeded him peacefully.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A rumour that Hoshang Shah had poisoned his father gave Muzaffar Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> a pretext for war. Muzaffar invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> late in 1406 and in 1407 reached Dhar, where Hoshang was wounded, defeated and forced back into the fort. When the siege proved difficult, Muzaffar proposed peace with an oath on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. Against his advisers' warnings, Hoshang met him several times and was arrested. Malwa was annexed by the Gujarat Sultanate under Muzaffar's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-khan">Nusrat Khan</a>, and Hoshang was taken captive to Gujarat.</p><p>Local soldiers and nobles soon drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-khan">Nusrat Khan</a> out and by December 1408 chose Hoshang's cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-khan-of-bengal">Musa Khan</a> as their leader at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>. Muzaffar then sent his grandson Ahmad Khan, who restored Hoshang at Dhar. Musa Khan refused to submit until Hoshang seized the surrounding districts to cut off the fort's revenue and key nobles, among them Malik Mughith, deserted him. Hoshang re-entered Mandu, made it his capital and appointed Malik Mughith his naib and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a>.</p><p>Hoshang kept friendly relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-khan-faruqi">Nasir Khan Faruqi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a>, a buffer against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, founded Hoshangabad on the Narbada to guard his south-eastern frontier and gained suzerainty over Kalpi by marrying his sister to Qadir Shah. No forced conversions or temple demolitions are recorded in his reign, and a Jain temple was built at Lalitpur in 1424. He founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a> and drew scholars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saints to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years a rivalry between his sons by two wives troubled the court. When Usman Khan revolted, Hoshang imprisoned the sons of his first wife, a daughter of Malik Ahmad Khan Farooqui of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a>, which cleared the way for Ghazni Khan, who later reigned as Muhammad Shah. According to Nizamuddin Ahmad, Hoshang suffered from diabetes. He fell gravely ill and died in July 1435 while being carried back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>, where he was buried the next day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hoshang Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Hoshang Shah</b> moved the capital from Dhar to the plateau fortress of Mandu, which he fortified and adorned.</blockquote><a href="/state/malwa-sultanate">Malwa Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Mandu, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.34,75.42&amp;year=1425" aria-label="Mandu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Hoshang Shah"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3859 5581h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3859 5581h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=22.34,75.42&amp;year=1425">What was here in 1425? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
